---
// src/pages/company.astro — Company page (v2 IA; replaces /about)
import PageLayout from '@layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import CTASection from '@components/CTASection.astro';
---

<PageLayout
  title="About FurnaceWatch"
  description="FurnaceWatch was built by an engineer who instrumented his own furnace after one too many failures — and realised the people who should know first are the companies that service it."
>
  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="section-sm bg-navy-50 border-b border-navy-200">
    <div class="container-fw text-center flex flex-col items-center gap-4 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <span class="section-eyebrow">Our story</span>
      <h1 class="section-title text-4xl sm:text-[44px]">The furnace was saying what was wrong. Nobody was listening.</h1>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Story -->
  <section class="section bg-white">
    <div class="container-fw max-w-3xl mx-auto flex flex-col gap-6 text-navy-600 leading-relaxed text-lg">
      <p>
        FurnaceWatch started with a furnace that kept failing. A dirty flame sensor one winter, a
        draft inducer the next. Each time, the technician diagnosed the problem in minutes and said
        the same thing: it had been acting up for a while.
      </p>
      <p>
        The information existed — in the sound of the inducer, the rhythm of the cycles, the heat
        call that went unanswered. It just wasn't reaching anyone. So our founder, an engineer,
        instrumented his own furnace and started listening.
      </p>
      <p>
        What became clear along the way is who actually needs that signal. Not the homeowner —
        they find out when the house gets cold either way. It's the service company that installed
        the furnace, warranted it, and put its name on the maintenance agreement. They take the
        6 AM call, roll the truck without a diagnosis, and eat the callback. They should know
        first, and they should know <em>what</em>.
      </p>
      <p>
        That's what FurnaceWatch is: a sensor your technician mounts on the furnace, and a portal
        that tells your office the failure mode the moment a heating cycle goes wrong. It doesn't
        predict the future and it doesn't replace your technicians — it hands them the diagnosis
        before the truck leaves.
      </p>
      <p>
        FurnaceWatch is founder-led and early. If you book a demo, you'll be talking to the person
        who designed the hardware, trained the detection, and built the portal — and who wants to
        hear exactly where it falls short for the way your company runs.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Values -->
  <section class="section bg-navy-50 border-t border-navy-200">
    <div class="container-fw flex flex-col gap-11">
      <div class="text-center">
        <span class="section-eyebrow">How we build</span>
      </div>
      <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4 gap-4 max-w-6xl mx-auto">
        {[
          { title: 'Diagnosis, not prediction', body: 'We tell you what failed the moment it fails. We don’t claim to see the future, and we won’t sell you copy that does.' },
          { title: 'Open evidence', body: 'Every alert carries the cycle behind it — what the sensor saw and when. No black boxes.' },
          { title: 'Built for technicians', body: 'The install has to fit inside a normal maintenance visit, or it doesn’t ship.' },
          { title: 'On the device', body: 'Detection runs on the sensor itself, so a Wi-Fi drop never means an unwatched furnace.' },
        ].map(v => (
          <div class="card p-6 flex flex-col gap-2.5">
            <h3 class="font-display font-bold text-base text-navy-900">{v.title}</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-navy-500 leading-relaxed">{v.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTASection />
</PageLayout>
